  abstract = {Despite advances in pixel-level crack segmentation using deep learning, the lack of quantitatively and qualitatively adequate data for model training is still a pressing concern. The primary reason for this challenge lies in the difficulty in data collection and annotation. Addressing that, we propose in this article a novel approach to synthesize crack images and corresponding annotations, taking into account the crack propagation and recent effective generative adversarial networks (GANs). The synthesized annotations address the painstaking human annotation, facilitating precise model training and evaluation. Additive noises are generated from the false positives of a thresholding technique to synthesize extraneous objects such as road elements and, more importantly, to remove repetitive background patches caused by the minority of crack pixels in the analyzed images. For evaluation, we augment 10 000 real-world crack images, synthesize 30 000 crack images with additive noise, and 30 000 noise-free ones from three different GANs to train selected segmentation models and then test them on images of the GAPs384 and OmniCrack30k datasets. Experimental results validate the effectiveness of our approach on all segmentation models, showing an improvement of up to 2.8 % in$F_\beta  $. The datasets are publicly accessible at SynthesizedCrackImages.},
}

