  abstract = {The clinical characteristics of epilepsy can be mimicked with several other disorders, including syncopal, behavioral, and psychogenic conditions. Thus, making an accurate diagnostic framework such as an EEG-enabled smart Electronic wearable for hospitals remains a challenging task, Moreover, most of the patients coming to hospitals for medical interventions are in their interictal state. Therefore, diagnosing epilepsy in the absence of prevalent features, such as interictal epileptic discharges (IEDs), makes it more complicated. Existing methods which employ spatial/temporal features and deep learning models have shown promise but remain limited by issues of nonstationarity, intra-intersubject variability, and signal heterogeneity. To address these shortcomings, we propose a new computational feature, termedht-PLV, which incorporates Gaussian-Sinc Adaptive Envelopes over the Hilbert transform to compute the phase locking value which improved overall robustness. Another major bottleneck in epilepsy research is the lack of research studies involving both epilepsy patients and mimickers. To overcome this, we introduce the Max Epilepsy–Mimicker dataset, comprising EEG recordings from 100 subjects (50 interictal epilepsy and 50 mimicker), collected under the ILAE-2017 guidelines. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposedht–PLV feature enables accurate discrimination between epilepsy patients and mimickers, achieving a test accuracy of 93.00% and an AUC of 98% on the Max },
}

