  howpublished = {International conference on Fuzzy Theory and Its Applications},
  annotation = {中文辅助译名：使用光谱和连接特征治疗轻度认知障碍的双阶段脑电图分类框架},
  abstract = {Early detection of mild cognitive impairment (MCI) is crucial for aging populations. Electroencephalography (EEG) provides a noninvasive, cost-effective approach for cognitive assessment. This study developed a novel two-stage EEG-based classification framework to distinguish normal controls (NC), MCI, and dementia patients using physiologically interpretable features. We proposed a two-stage random forest framework was implemented: the first stage used power spectral density (PSD) features to classify dementia vs. non-dementia, while the second stage employed coherence features to distinguish NC vs. MCI. The results showed that alpha and theta band power, along with frontal-related coherences, can effectively distinguish MCI from other groups. Based on the proposed two-stage framework, the classifier for dementia vs. non-dementia achieved an accuracy of 89.53 %, while the classifier for NC vs. MCI reached 71.15 %, indicating that EEG features hold strong potential as biomarkers for the early diagnosis and monitoring of MCI and dementia. The classifiers provided in this study indicate that these selected EEG features can be effectively integrated into AI models for early detection of MCI patients and the investigation of cognitive deterioration. This research enhances our comprehension of EEG's role in the early screening of neurodegenerative disorders, especially in the early detection and tracking of situations such as MCI, establishing a foundation for future diagnostic an},
}

